{
  /*
   * `service/test/` — the one directory in this package that is allowed a host.
   *
   * There is exactly one file in it, and it is here because of what it does
   * rather than because it is a test: `coverage.test.ts` reads the suite off
   * disk to prove no registered tool goes unexercised, and `node:fs` is banned
   * everywhere `check-platform.mjs` looks. Rather than weaken that ban or fake
   * the read, the file sits outside both guards' roots — `kg/` and `data/` — and
   * gets its own project, which is the only one in the package with
   * `"types": ["node"]`.
   *
   * The `.test.ts` files under `kg/` and `data/` are NOT here. They are compiled
   * by the projects that own the code they test, under that code's lib and
   * types, which is the property that makes them evidence about the shipped
   * layer rather than about a test environment. Only a test that needs a
   * platform belongs in this project, and needing one should stay rare enough to
   * argue about.
   *
   * A consequence worth knowing: this project reaches `kg/core`, `kg/repo` and
   * `kg/tools` through the imports below, so those sources are also compiled
   * here, with `node` in the types. That is a WEAKER program over them than
   * tsconfig.core.json's, and it is harmless only because the stricter one still
   * runs — `tsc -b` builds every project in the solution and a `process` in
   * `kg/tools` fails there. Do not read a green build of this project alone as
   * saying anything about those layers.
   */
  "extends": "./tsconfig.base.json",
  "references": [{ "path": "./tsconfig.storage.json" }],
  "compilerOptions": {
    "tsBuildInfoFile": "./node_modules/.tmp/tsconfig.test.tsbuildinfo",
    "lib": ["ES2023"],
    "types": ["node"],
    "disableSourceOfProjectReferenceRedirect": true
  },
  "include": ["test", "types/portable-globals.d.ts"]
}
